Hello Dr. Partridge,

My name is Rodrigo Castro, founder of Listening.bio. Given your work in urban bird conservation and science-based biodiversity protection in New York City, I am writing to ask whether you would consider advising a narrowly scoped validation pilot.

Listening.bio ingests environmental recordings, runs BirdNET, preserves raw model outputs and processing context, supports human review, and exports georeferenced evidence. The workflow has been verified with public audio, but it has not yet been scientifically validated as a field-monitoring method. That is the next step.

I propose a 30-day study across three to five NYC sites using 50 to 100 recordings. Listening.bio would provide the software, processing, provenance controls, maps, and exports. I am seeking limited scientific guidance on the sampling design, a defensible annotation and error-analysis method, and review of a representative detection subset.

The system treats AI predictions as reviewable candidates, not ecological truth. A successful pilot would produce a reusable protocol, an annotated validation sample, a transparent error report, and preliminary evidence for a partner-led grant application. Listening.bio would cover the technology work for the initial pilot.

If the methods question is relevant to your work, would you be willing to review the one-page protocol by email and identify the two or three scientific requirements that would make the pilot defensible? If this is outside your current scope, I would be grateful for an email referral to a colleague or graduate researcher for whom it is a better fit. The live evidence demonstration is at https://listening.bio.

